{
 "status": "A13 second slice \u2014 recommendations, ranking and history controls. SRC-03 does NOT flip.",
 "scope": "Co-visitation -> related cards, the home rail and its sources, search filters/paging/ranking, search-history controls and their authorization. Watch-history controls were proven in A12 and are not repeated. Autosuggest thresholds, suggestion bans and the trending gates are the FIRST slice (docs/evidence/a13-suggest-trending.json) and are not re-certified here.",
 "core_revision": "origin/main 425f146 + PR #168 (a13/recommendations-history)",
 "search_revision": "origin/main e8a1f76 (UNMODIFIED \u2014 run as a dependency, asserted directly)",
 "frontend_revision": "origin/main a1b6db8 + PR #165 (a13/recommendations-history)",
 "pull_requests": {
  "vidra-core#168": "fix(search): has_more describes the client page; personalized is mode-gated; claim-owner returns the discovery controls",
  "vidra-user#165": "fix(home): HomeRecommendationsRail waits for the session to settle",
  "meta": "this evidence + release-readiness section",
  "order": "core#168 first (it changes what the rails report), then user#165, then the meta evidence PR"
 },
 "environment": "Disposable LOCAL lab, native. Homebrew PostgreSQL 16 + Redis 8. vidra-core built from the branch on 127.0.0.1:8088 (VIDRA_ENV=development, VIDRA_ROLE=all, local blob storage, ffmpeg present, throwaway database migrated 0 -> 129). vidra-search built from main on 127.0.0.1:8090 against its own throwaway database at schema 16 and its own Redis logical database (flushed before seeding), wired to core through SEARCH_SERVICE_URL + a lab-only SEARCH_INTERNAL_SECRET. The vidra-user PRODUCTION build served on 127.0.0.1:3100. Shipped rate limits left ON at defaults (120 general / 10 auth per IP per minute) and the harness paced itself: zero 429 responses. Synthetic actors only; the owner claimed through the real first-run bootstrap. Torn down at the end.",
 "browser": "Chromium via Playwright 1.62.1 (headless, real browser, production frontend build, one browser context per actor, 1440x1000). No route interception anywhere \u2014 responses are only observed. No fake search server. Hard reloads where stated.",
 "actors": {
  "M": "mallory \u2014 instance owner, claimed through the real first-run bootstrap",
  "U1..U3": "alice, bob, carol \u2014 three signed-in users; each watched the SAME pair (A1 then A2) in one session",
  "U4": "dave \u2014 watched C1 -> P -> U alone in one session (the single-user co-visitation probe)",
  "U5": "erin \u2014 turned all three search/personalisation controls OFF",
  "U6": "frank \u2014 registered late and never watched anything (the cold-start probe)",
  "anonymous": "no session cookie, no Authorization header"
 },
 "fixture": {
  "channels": "zephqar (alice), mirvane (bob), quolbex (carol), traskil (dave), vandrel (erin)",
  "videos": "25 real uploads through POST /channels/{h}/videos + POST /videos/{id}/file (2 s 320x180 clips), all published, all indexed by the real outbox -> /internal/v1/events path. A1..A3, B1..B2, C1, D1..D2, E1..E3, F1..F12 public; P (Quolbex Vault Private) and U (Quolbex Vault Unlisted) created public, co-visited, then flipped to private/unlisted so their neighbour edges outlive their eligibility. E3 backdated 120 days in core and re-indexed for the freshness probe. Every description carries the token 'plexoid', which matches no catalogue text."
 },
 "shipped_rules_as_shipped": {
  "co_visitation": "covis_rollup worker, cadence SEARCH_COVIS_INTERVAL, DEFAULT 15 MINUTES (not 60 s like the aggregates and trending sweepers). It folds behavior_events of type video.play_started / video.meaningful_watch into search.co_watch, and search.result_clicked into search.co_search, pairing each NEW event only with EARLIER events in the SAME session_id within SEARCH_COVIS_WINDOW_SECONDS, DEFAULT 3600 s. It then rebuilds search.item_neighbors (model_version 'covis-v1') from scratch: shrunk cosine raw = cooc/sqrt(totI*totJ), shrunk = raw * cooc/(cooc+lambda) with SEARCH_COVIS_LAMBDA DEFAULT 10, blended 0.7*co_watch + 0.3*co_search, top SEARCH_COVIS_TOP_M DEFAULT 100 neighbours per item. THERE IS NO DISTINCT-USER FLOOR ON CO-VISITATION: the only filter is score > 0. Autosuggest and trending both enforce minimum_query_user_count = 3; the co-visitation index enforces nothing.",
  "related_source": "vidra-search dispatches GET /internal/v1/recommendations/related on the instance search_mode. simple (THE SHIPPED DEFAULT) runs relatedSimple: up to 2 recent same-channel videos, then tag/category/language overlap, then a popular fill \u2014 CO-VISITATION IS NOT CONSULTED AT ALL. advanced runs relatedAdvanced: item_neighbors for the seed (reason co_watch, priority 3, the highest) union the simple sets union the session's co-watch seed union a popular fill, reranked by base + 0.6*neighbour affinity + 0.4*channel affinity + 0.3*freshness (30-day half-life) with a 0.5 novelty penalty, MMR-diversified at lambda 0.7, a per-channel cap of 2, and a deterministic epsilon=0.1 exploration slot.",
  "home_rail_source": "GET /api/v1/recommendations/home -> vidra-search /internal/v1/recommendations/home. simple: the gated Redis trending list (trend:v:top, the sweeper's post-gate output, cached 5 min), falling back to a SQL HN-gravity query when it is empty, round-robin interleaved with fresh and popular under a per-channel cap of 2. Personalization is IGNORED in this mode. advanced adds, for a personalized signed-in caller, NeighborsForUserWatches (co-visitation of everything the user watched, weighted by the decayed watch projection) and EXCLUDES the last 200 videos they watched. Core hydrates the ranked ids under the canonical predicate and falls back to its own trending feed when the service errors or returns nothing.",
  "cold_start": "A brand-new signed-in user with no watch projection gets the generic list \u2014 the same items as anonymous \u2014 because every personalized candidate generator keys off user_watch_projection. In advanced mode the response still reports personalized=true for them, so the rail is headed 'For you' over the generic list; that is cold start as shipped, not a bug in the flag.",
  "live_ranker": "search_mode DEFAULTS TO simple, so the live ranker is vidra-search's SearchSimple SQL: score = 0.5*ts_rank_cd(tsv, websearch_to_tsquery(q)) + 0.2*similarity(lower(title), q) + 0.1*(title exact 1.0 + channel exact 0.5 + tag exact 0.5) + 0.1*ln(1+views)/20 + 0.1*exp(-ln2 * age_days / 30), ordered score DESC, published_at DESC NULLS LAST, video_id. Recall unions full-text, trigram title similarity (candidate threshold 0.3) and exact tag/channel match. The internal/experiment + LightGBM shadow path is NOT live in simple mode and was not exercised.",
  "search_routing": "searchServiceCanRank: the vidra-search path is taken ONLY for the default relevance sort with none of duration/publish-window/tag-set filters active. tag, category, language and license go to the service; duration_min/max, published_after/before, tags_all_of/tags_one_of and any non-relevance sort run on core's local SQL, on both sides of the healthy/unhealthy line. `total` is always CORE's own per-viewer count.",
  "history_write_rule": "A durable user_search_history row is written only for an event carrying allow_history=true AND a signed-in user_id. allow_history is set by core on POST /search/events only (instanceSearchHistoryEnabled && authed && prefs.History). The search.submitted that GET /videos/search emits itself never sets it, so an API-only client's searches are retained in query_log and NEVER appear on that user's search-history page. Confirmed again here.",
  "history_delete": "DELETE /me/search-history: enqueue the durable user.history_deleted FIRST, then erase core's own search_outbox rows for the user, then proxy to the service, which in ONE transaction deletes user_search_history and NULLs user_id in query_log and behavior_events. DELETE /me/search-history/{query} is an honest proxy only: a hard delete of that one history row, with no durable event and no core-side erasure (documented in the handler \u2014 user.history_deleted has no query field, and core stores raw text while the path parameter is the service's normalized form). Nothing recreates a deleted row except the user searching again."
 },
